# /new-system-spec

## What This Does

Writes an end-to-end spec in `spec/system/` that drives the app the way a user
does: visit, fill in, click, assert on what is visible. Sets up the browser
driver once if the project has none.

## Usage

```
/new-system-spec "user edits their profile"
/new-system-spec "sign in with wrong password shows an error"
```

## How It Works

### 1. Check the existing setup

```bash
ls spec/system spec/support 2>/dev/null
grep -rn "driven_by\|register_driver" spec/ | head
grep -n "cuprite\|playwright\|selenium" Gemfile
```

Use the driver the project already registers. Only when there is none, set one
up (step 2).

### 2. Driver setup (once per project)

{% if js %}
**Cuprite** (default choice: drives Chrome over CDP, no chromedriver/Selenium):

```ruby
# Gemfile, group :test
gem "capybara"
gem "cuprite"

# spec/support/system.rb
require "capybara/cuprite"

Capybara.register_driver(:cuprite) do |app|
  Capybara::Cuprite::Driver.new(app, window_size: [1400, 1000], headless: ENV["HEADFUL"].blank?)
end

RSpec.configure do |config|
  config.before(:each, type: :system) { driven_by :rack_test }
  config.before(:each, type: :system, js: true) { driven_by :cuprite }
end
```

**Playwright** (alternative, when the team also needs Firefox/WebKit): add
`capybara-playwright-driver`, install a browser with `npx playwright install chromium`,
and register it:

```ruby
Capybara.register_driver(:playwright) do |app|
  Capybara::Playwright::Driver.new(app, browser_type: :chromium, headless: true)
end
# then: driven_by :playwright for js: true specs
```
{% else %}
This app has no JavaScript layer, so every system spec runs on `rack_test`
(fast, no browser):

```ruby
# spec/support/system.rb
RSpec.configure do |config|
  config.before(:each, type: :system) { driven_by :rack_test }
end
```
{% endif %}

### 3. Sign-in helper

Use what the project has. With the Rails 8 authentication generator there is no
test helper, so sign in through the form:

```ruby
# spec/support/system_auth_helpers.rb
module SystemAuthHelpers
  def sign_in_as(user, password: "password")
    visit new_session_path
    fill_in "email_address", with: user.email_address
    fill_in "password", with: password
    click_button "Sign in"
  end
end
RSpec.configure { |c| c.include SystemAuthHelpers, type: :system }
```

With Devise: `include Warden::Test::Helpers` and `login_as(user)` (no form needed).

### 4. Write the spec

```ruby
# spec/system/profile_editing_spec.rb
require "rails_helper"

RSpec.describe "Profile editing", type: :system{{ ', js: true' if js else '' }} do
  let(:user) { create(:user) }
  let!(:profile) { create(:profile, user:, name: "Old name") }

  before { sign_in_as(user) }

  it "saves the new name" do
    visit edit_profile_path(profile)
    fill_in "Name", with: "Ada Lovelace"
    click_button "Update Profile"

    expect(page).to have_text("Profile updated")
    expect(page).to have_text("Ada Lovelace")
  end

  it "shows validation errors" do
    visit edit_profile_path(profile)
    fill_in "Name", with: ""
    click_button "Update Profile"

    expect(page).to have_css("[role=alert]", text: "can't be blank")
  end
end
```

### 5. Run it

```bash
bundle exec rspec spec/system/profile_editing_spec.rb
```

## Rules

- One user goal per file; one behavior per `it`
- Find by what users see: labels, button text, roles (`have_button`, `have_field`); avoid CSS-class selectors
{% if js %}
- Add `js: true` only when the flow needs JavaScript ({% if rt == 'hotwire' %}Turbo Frames/Streams, {% endif %}Stimulus); everything else stays on `rack_test`
- Never `sleep`: Capybara matchers wait (`have_text`, `have_no_css`); raise `Capybara.default_max_wait_time` only if the app is genuinely slow
{% endif %}
- Assert the outcome (text, URL, record count) after the action, not intermediate DOM
- Keep test data in factories; no fixtures in system specs
